WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.319
Actress Sofia Boutella might seem like a newcomer,
with her eerie turn as Princess Ahmanet in

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.460
the 2017 reboot of The Mummy launching her
into the Hollywood mainstream.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.600
But the Algerian-French actress has actually
been working her way into the limelight for

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.320
years, and has amassed a number of credits
that may make her face familiar to Mummy-watchers

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.080
across the world.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.780
Here's where you might've seen Boutella before.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.360
Those Nike ads

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.260
Boutella started her career in the arts as
a dancer and was known for her choreography

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.600
skills, particularly in the hip-hop genre.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.220
She achieved a commercial breakthrough in
the mid-aughts when she starred in a series

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.040
of "Make Yourself"-themed advertisements for
Nike.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.540
That exposure would wind up landing her backup
dancing gigs with artists like Madonna, who

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.580
kept her touring for the better part of a
decade.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.900
"And then I started dancing with her and,
uh, she gave me a pair of heels for the first

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:52.770
time asking me if I ever danced in heels and
I said 'No' and she said 'There's a beginning

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:53.360
for everything.'"

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:54.600
Michael Jackson

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.060
Boutella's work with Madonna brought her to
the attention of the biggest pop star in the

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:04.160
world, Michael Jackson, who offered her a
chance to star in his This Is It concert series.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.220
She told Dancing Times, "I spoke to [Jackson]
on the phone.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.400
He called me, he said, 'You're an amazing
dancer.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.040
God bless you.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.160
I really want you on my show.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.280
And I said, 'I will do my best.'

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.960
Then I was on the phone to his stage director,
Kenny Ortega, and he told me: 'When you're

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.980
done with Madonna, come and join us,' and
I said, 'OK, I will.'

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.280
And then he passed."

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.990
Although she never got to share the stage
with the King of Pop, she did have the chance

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.990
to collaborate with the singer posthumously,
when she starred as the lead in the music

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.060
video for his single "Hollywood Tonight."

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.460
StreetDance 2

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.060
In 2012, Boutella made the jump to movies
with the film StreetDance 2.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:48.520
She played a dancer named Eva, who specializes
in the tango and salsa—something Boutella

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:49.680
didn't have much experience with.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.680
But she still felt a kindred spirit with her
character.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.820
She told All Street Dance, "Eva is a Latin
dancer, and she is totally abandoning herself

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.960
with those hip-hop dancers … she is giving
herself this chance for this alternative,

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.540
to learn something else, to learn something
that is completely different and give herself

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:10.550
to this world that she's not used to, and
she's taking risks.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.160
… I think this is something I can recognize
of myself.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.420
Not with hip-hop or Latin dance, but with
life … I see things, and I want to go there,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.240
even if it costs something because you're
letting go of something that you know so much."

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.340
Indeed, Boutella would eventually set aside
her ambitions as a dancer to pursue her screen

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:27.840
career.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.940
Kingsman: The Secret Service

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.840
In Matthew Vaughn's 2014 hit Kingsman: The
Secret Service, Boutella starred as Gazelle,

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.660
a role that was originally written for a man.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.360
But when Vaughn got a look at Boutella's Nike
ads, he decided to redesign the characters.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.800
"I thought, you know what, we can make this
quite sexy and stylish and if you've got blades

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:49.950
like that, you don't have to be strong.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.380
You just have to be agile and precise, which
she was."

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.960
Kingsman was Boutella's first big Hollywood
production, an experience she described as

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:58.600
surreal.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.780
"When I arrived and I see Sam Jackson and
Mark Hamill here on set, it was my first day

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.380
shooting and I couldn't believe I was there.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.240
But yet I had to focus and do all of the work,
you know."

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:09.960
Star Trek Beyond

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.540
In the 2016 sci-fi blockbuster Star Trek Beyond,
the third installment to the rebooted Star

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:20.900
Trek film series, Boutella starred as Jaylah,
a character that was famously inspired by

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.740
Jennifer Lawrence.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.720
Although Boutella would hardly be recognizable
beneath her thick facial prosthetics, she'd

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.650
make a more personality-oriented mark on the
character's development.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:38.300
"Jaylah has this sense of innocence but also
this quality of strength that is not manufactured."

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.900
Boutella's choreography skills came in handy
during her many fight scenes, earning her

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.140
the respect of co-stars like Simon Pegg, who
described her as "the real deal."

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.280
"She comes from a very strong race of aliens.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.280
Physically, extremely strong."

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.880
"She's incredibly tough, independent, resourceful..."

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.160
"You help me, and I help you."

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:57.660
Atomic Blonde

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:03.060
Film stardom may not have been her first ambition,
but Sofia Boutella is moving at warp speed

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:03.600
now.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:08.480
In addition to landing a pivotal role in The
Mummy, she also snagged the part of a French

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.900
spy named Delphine in the action flick Atomic
Blonde opposite Oscar winner Charlize Theron.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.040
Boutella described her love scenes with Theron
as "very technical," telling USA Today, "It's

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.860
not as glamorous as you think … You want
it all to translate in the film, so that it's

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:25.360
very hot and steamy."

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.960
Boutella's Atomic Blonde work should only
increase her exposure to a quickly growing

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.640
audience—and without requiring her to wreak
undead havoc, too.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.400
While her Mummy character is a core component
of Universal's Dark Universe, Sofia Boutella's

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.800
star looks nothing but bright.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.839
Thanks for watching!

